{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Janitor skill validation redesign - decisions so far (pending two research reports). APPROVED: (1) intent check folded into the FIRST validation pass, triggered subject-scoped rather than verb-scoped - item 16 proposed an ADDITION ('persist the close reason') that was a reversal only at the subject level, so a removal-verb trigger would have missed it; queries are git log -S, the originating commit's rebar-ticket trailer, ADR grep, and comments citing ticket ids. (2) Proposer decorrelation by QUESTION, not just reading list: Proposer A gets web search and answers 'how does the community do this', Proposer B answers 'how does this project do this' from the record; both grounded in the code. This covers the remediation-property error class - '_-prefixing is safe' was refuted by ruff#10409, which Proposer A would have surfaced at proposal time. (3) 'Is the harm reachable' sub-question, requiring a concrete triggering input/state; un-demonstrable is classified HARDENING not DEFECT (would have downgraded the B905 claim). REJECTED: a basis/confidence-provenance field. DECIDED: no second validation pass and no validation at synthesis - the synthesizer is the orchestrator and therefore the invested party, which is the failure mode observed this run. (4) Divergence between proposers drives an additional targeted research round asking WHY, specifically whether the community has EXPLICITLY REJECTED the project's approach; divergence remains a finding reported with both positions and their evidence, never a synthesized winner."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786056524613782001,"uuid":"40f4128d-87d6-40ed-b31a-00176eeadfe1"}